  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "A laboratory is tracking the rate at which a chemical is being produced in a reaction. The rate $r(t)$ at which the chemical is produced is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+4,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in milligrams per hour, and $0<t<5$ is the time in hours since $1\\,\\text{pm}.$\n\nBetween $t=1$ and $t=3$ hours,\n{{field-1}} milligrams were produced.",
  "worked_solution": "We're given the **rate of change** of chemical production $r(t).$ To find the **net amount** of chemical produced by the reaction between the times $t=1$ and $t=3,$ we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}t & ={(\\frac{2}{4}{t}^{4}-\\frac{3}{3}{t}^{3}+4t)|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{4}-{t}^{3}+4t)|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2}(3)^{4}-(3)^{3}+4(3))-(\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{4}-(1)^{3}+4(1)) \\\\  & =(\\frac{81}{2}-27+12)-(\\frac{1}{2}-1+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{2}-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, between $t=1$ and $t=3$ hours, $22$ milligrams were produced.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
